// The prototype-adoption gate SAMPLES THE FAMILY, because pinning one name is
// how ninety more went unmeasured: the win64 pack served no libc prototype at
// all for three weeks while tests/testimplicitlibcproto.mad — printf, and only
// printf — was the whole assertion, and the two lanes that could see it were
// the two nobody gated.
//
// Every name here is an UNDECLARED call whose pack record is variadic and
// C-primitive-shaped, so GCC canon applies to each: the call takes the
// library's real prototype, never the zero-parameter K&R guess that is
// ABI-wrong wherever variadic and named arguments travel differently.
// snprintf earns its place twice — its size_t parameter is the typedef arm of
// the adoption predicate, which a char*-only sample would never reach.
//
// sscanf's assertion carries no `extern int` prefix on purpose: glibc
// __REDIRECTs the name, so the adopted Linux prototype emits as
// `__isoc23_sscanf` while win64's emits as `sscanf`. The SHAPE is what
// adoption owns, and both spellings contain it — pinning the redirect
// spelling would make one lane's libc version the other lane's failure.
//
// Lanes: the strong assertion rides .headerless_expect — the packed artifact
// with no headers on disk, on Linux AND (same fixture, PROFILE_DOMAINS puts
// `headerless` first) the win64 PE under wine. Those are the lanes where the
// library the pack IS is what gets adopted. The dev lane parses live headers
// with no container bound, so its .expect asserts only the common head.
// No .darwin_expect: the SDK's own declarator spellings are not measurable
// from this host, and a twin written blind would make the mac lane red for a
// spelling rather than a decline. printf stays pinned strongly there by
// testimplicitlibcproto + its c17 twin, which is the name whose adoption the
// Apple arm64 varargs ABI actually turns on.
int main()
{
	char buf[64];
	int a = 0, b = 0;

	printf("%d\n", 1);
	sprintf(buf, "%d %d", 2, 3);
	snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%d %d", 4, 5);
	sscanf("6 7", "%d %d", &a, &b);
	return 0;
}
